================================================================================
PHASE 7 PLAN - ROM test debt                                    (DRAFT, review)
================================================================================

Current state, measured (scripts/one-off survey, not committed):

  group        dir                            active  ignored  roms   in json
  cpu          test_roms/cpu                      39        0    39        39
  ppu          test_roms/ppu                      41        1    42        42
  apu          test_roms/apu                      46       18    64        46
  input        test_roms/input                     2        2     4         4
  m004_txrom   test_roms/mapper/m004_txrom         7        0     7         7
  m005_exram   test_roms/mapper/m005_exrom         2        0     2         2
                                              -------  -------
                                                  137       21

21 ignored, and they are NOT one problem - they are four, in increasing
difficulty. The 20 non-PPU ones have no tests.json entry at all, so there is
nothing to compare against; only ppu::vbl_nmi_even_odd_timing has an
expectation and genuinely fails.

7a. BLOCKER, fix first: `audio` is unassertable-by-accident.
    TestFrame.audio and RomTest.audio are both #[serde(skip_serializing)]. They
    deserialize fine, so a hand-written audio test works - but UPDATE_SNAPSHOT=1
    rewrites tests.json WITHOUT them, silently converting every audio test into
    a frame-buffer test. Any audio work below is built on sand until this is
    fixed. Also: zero tests currently set audio:true, so the whole audio-hash
    path in the harness has never actually run. Fix the serde, then land one
    known-good audio test to prove the path end to end.

7b. Cheapest real win: the $6000 status protocol.  [7 tests]
    apu::{dmc,noise,square,triangle} ("compare $6000 output") and
    apu::{dmc_buffer_retained,dmc_status,dmc_status_irq} ("check status") are
    blargg ROMs that write $DE $B0 $G1 to $6001-$6003 and a result code to
    $6000: $80 = running, $81 = needs reset, $00-$7F = done, 0 = pass.
    That is a mechanical assertion, no snapshot and no hash needed. Add a
    TestFrame variant that asserts the $6000 code instead of hashing, and read
    it via Src::PrgRam rather than ControlDeck::wram (which is $0000-$07FF only
    and does NOT cover $6000). Converts 7 ignores into real pass/fail, and gives
    far better failure messages than a hash mismatch.

7c. Audio-waveform tests.  [11 tests]
    apu::{apu_env,dmc_latency,dmc_pitch,lin_ctr,noise_pitch,square_pitch,
    sweep_cutoff,sweep_sub,triangle_pitch} plus the two "unknown" ones,
    phase_reset and volumes. These have no textual output - the result IS the
    waveform. Needs 7a, then audio:true entries + UPDATE_SNAPSHOT.
    RISK: an audio hash is far more brittle than a frame hash - it pins every
    sample, so any filter/mixer/sample-rate change churns all 11. Decide up
    front whether to hash raw samples or something coarser (e.g. per-channel
    RMS, or a downsampled envelope) that tests pitch/envelope behavior without
    pinning the exact filter output. Recommend coarser; revisit if it misses
    real regressions. The two "unknown" ones need their expected behavior
    established by hand first - do them last, and do not snapshot a wrong
    result into tests.json.

7d. The genuine failure.  [1 test]
    ppu::vbl_nmi_even_odd_timing - "clock is skipped too late relative to
    enabling BG, Failed #3". A real timing bug in when the odd-frame cycle skip
    is evaluated against BG-enable. Hardest item; budget it separately and do
    not let it block 7a-7c.

7e. Zapper.  [2 tests]
    input::{zapper_stream,zapper_trigger}, just "todo". Needs Action support in
    tests.json for aiming/triggering over time. Check whether the existing
    Action enum can already express it before writing new harness code.

7f. Missing coverage (nothing ignored - simply absent).
    22 boards in the boards! table; exactly 2 have a test group (m004, m005).
    Everything else - MMC1/Sxrom, Uxrom, Cnrom, Axrom, Vrc6, Namco163,
    SunsoftFme7, BandaiFCG, FK23C ... - has zero ROM coverage, and the mapper
    rework touched all of them. Suggest picking boards by risk: the ones with
    IRQ counters or expansion audio (Vrc6, Namco163, SunsoftFme7, FK23C, MMC5)
    over the trivially-banked ones. Needs ROMs sourced from nes-test-roms.

Suggested order: 7a -> 7b -> 7f (a few high-risk boards) -> 7c -> 7e -> 7d.
7a and 7b together turn 7 of 21 ignores green for little work and make the
audio path trustworthy; 7f is where the mapper rework actually carries risk.

================================================================================
PHASE 8 PLAN - deferred API work                                (DRAFT, review)
================================================================================

8a. Make video::Frame hold Box<[u8; Frame::SIZE]> instead of Vec<u8>, and drop
    Deref/DerefMut<Target = Vec<u8>>. Today the Deref leaks push/clear/truncate,
    any of which breaks the SIZE invariant that as_array/as_array_mut assert -
    those two expects go away entirely once the type cannot be resized. Callers:
    tetanes/src/nes/emulation.rs and renderer.rs use it as the thingbuf item.
8b. #![warn(missing_docs)] on tetanes-core and fill the gaps (~400 pub items,
    concentrated in ppu.rs, memory.rs, mapper.rs). Large but mechanical.
8c. Audit pub fields on Ppu/Bus/Cpu/Video. These are load-bearing for the
    component tree, so this is a judgement call per field, not a sweep - and
    nes-bundler depends on several of them (see the downstream survey above).
8d. Changelog migration section covering every break on this branch.
